+1.1 Fix $n\ge1$. In any finite tensor sum, all factor vectors lie in a finite-dimensional subspace $E\subseteq H$. Choose a finite orthonormal basis $(f_j)$ of $E$ by [A4]. Multilinearity expands the sum in the elementary tensors $f_{j_1}\otimes\cdots\otimes f_{j_n}$; the product form makes these tensors orthonormal. Their linear independence follows by applying the multilinear coordinate functionals $(x_1,\ldots,x_n)\mapsto\prod_{r=1}^n\langle x_r,f_{j_r}\rangle$, which descend to the quotient and extract their coefficients. Thus the form is positive definite on every finite tensor span, so it defines an inner-product norm on the algebraic tensor product. Under Countable Choice, [A6] supplies its metric completion, the compatible Banach structure and the extended inner product; this is the Hilbert tensor power $H^{\otimes n}$. If $H$ is finite-dimensional, a single finite orthonormal basis of $H$ gives a finite orthonormal basis of the entire algebraic tensor product. For any Cauchy sequence there, each of the finitely many coefficients is Cauchy in $\mathbb C$; its unique limit exists by [A7], and the resulting finite coefficient vector is the norm limit. Hence that algebraic tensor product is already Hilbert and may itself be used as its completion, without Countable Choice. For $n=1$, $H$ itself is a completion of its algebraic tensor power; under [A6], extending the identity in both directions identifies the constructed completion with $H$. [A1, A4, A6, A7, construct, algebra]
 
